      <!-- TABLE 2: Physical Probe Gas Constants -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        Adsorptive Probe Gas Constants and IUPAC Cross-Sectional Areas
      </h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        Selection of the appropriate probe gas is governed by pore aperture size, polar functionality, and thermal equilibration considerations:
      </p>

      <div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <table style="width: 100%; min-width: 650px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.85rem; background: #ffffff;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: #f1f5f9; text-align: left; color: #334155; font-size: 0.8rem; border-bottom: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1;">
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Probe Gas</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Boiling Temp</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Area Factor (&sigma;)</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Conversion Factor</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Primary Analytical Domain</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700;">Nitrogen (N₂)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">77.35 K</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">0.162 nm²</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0284c7;">4.353 m²/cm³</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; color: #475569;">Universal standard for mesoporous silicas, carbons, and catalysts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0; background: #fafafa;">
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700;">Argon (Ar)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">87.27 K</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">0.142 nm²</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0284c7;">3.815 m²/cm³</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; color: #475569;">Recommended for microporous zeolites and polar MOFs (zero quadrupole moment).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700;">Carbon Dioxide (CO₂)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">273.15 K</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">0.170 nm²</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0284c7;">4.568 m²/cm³</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; color: #475569;">Ultra-micropores (&lt; 0.7 nm); overcomes kinetic diffusion limitations at 77 K.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700;">Krypton (Kr)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">77.35 K</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">0.152 nm²</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0284c7;">4.084 m²/cm³</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; color: #475569;">Low-surface-area materials (&lt; 1.0 m²/g); vapor pressure is ~1/350th of N₂.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        The Rouquerol Criteria: Resolving Negative C-Constants in Microporous Solids
      </h3>
      <p>
        In microporous materials, pore condensation and volume filling happen at very low relative pressures (<em>P/P<sub>0</sub> &lt; 0.05</em>). Fitting the conventional linear range (<em>0.05 &ndash; 0.30</em>) causes severe thermodynamic errors: the y-intercept becomes negative, yielding a mathematically nonsensical negative C-constant.
      </p>
      <p>
        To prevent these systematic errors, researchers apply the <strong>Rouquerol consistency criteria</strong>:
      </p>
      <ol style="padding-left: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.8;">
        <li><strong>Monotonic Increase Criterion:</strong> The transformed quantity <strong>V<sub>a</sub>(1 &minus; P/P₀)</strong> must continuously increase with relative pressure. Any experimental point collected after the peak of <em>V<sub>a</sub>(1 &minus; P/P₀)</em> reflects multilayer or pore filling and must be removed from the regression.</li>
        <li><strong>Positive Intercept Requirement:</strong> The regression y-intercept (<em>i</em>) must be strictly greater than zero, guaranteeing an energetically valid net heat of adsorption constant (<em>C &gt; 0</em>).</li>
        <li><strong>Monolayer Range Location:</strong> The calculated monolayer capacity <em>V<sub>m</sub></em> must correspond to a relative pressure <em>P/P₀</em> that falls within the chosen linear fitting range.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- BENCHTOP TROUBLESHOOTING GUIDE -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        Benchtop Analytical Troubleshooting Matrix
      </h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        Common operational issues encountered during volumetric gas physisorption and their corrections:
      </p>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 300px), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #b91c1c; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;">
            <span>⚠️</span> Symptom: Negative C-Constant
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.84rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Upper pressure points exceed the monolayer limit, or microporous pore-filling is included in a mesopore fitting range.<br />
            <strong>Fix:</strong> Review the <em>V<sub>a</sub>(1 &minus; P/P₀)</em> column. Delete all isotherm points with relative pressures above the maximum value.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #d97706; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;">
            <span>⚠️</span> Symptom: Low Cell Sensitivity Warning (&lt; 2.0 m²)
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.84rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Insufficient adsorbent mass loaded into the analysis cell for pressure transducers to distinguish from free-space volume.<br />
            <strong>Fix:</strong> Increase sample loading mass using the relation <em>m<sub>min</sub> &ge; 2.0 / S<sub>expected</sub></em>, or switch probe gas to Krypton (Kr).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #0284c7; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;">
            <span>⚠️</span> Symptom: Thermal Collapse During Degas
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.84rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            <strong>Root Cause:</strong> Excessive outgassing temperature leads to solvent capillary collapse or decomposition of coordinate linkers (common in MOFs).<br />
            <strong>Fix:</strong> Verify framework thermal stability by TGA first. For delicate frameworks, use supercritical CO₂ activation or dynamic vacuum at 80°C–100°C for 16 h.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WORKED LABORATORY CASE STUDIES -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        Worked Laboratory Case Studies
      </h3>
      
      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #0284c7;">Case 1: Mesoporous Silica (MCM-41)</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.82rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            Evaluated in the standard range (P/P₀ = 0.062–0.252) with N₂ at 77 K (σ = 0.162 nm²). Linear slope s = 0.0039, intercept i = 0.00049. Derived V<sub>m</sub> = 227.8 cm³/g, C = 8.96. Resulting S<sub>BET</sub> = <strong>991.6 m²/g</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: #0284c7;">Case 2: Microporous MOF (HKUST-1)</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.82rem; color: #475569; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            Pore filling occurs below 0.05. Using Rouquerol criteria, the range is restricted to P/P₀ = 0.008–0.052. V<sub>a</sub>(1 &minus; P/P₀) increases monotonically, yielding C = 142.6 and an accurate S<sub>BET</sub> of <strong>1640.2 m²/g</strong> without negative intercept errors.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ ACCORDION SECTION -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Gas Physisorption &amp; Porosity
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How does a surface area and porosity analyzer perform N2 BET physisorption measurements?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            A surface area and porosity analyzer operates by dosing precise increments of nitrogen gas (N2) onto a degassed solid sample at cryogenic temperatures (77.35 K) and measuring equilibrium adsorption volumes across a linear relative pressure range (P/P0 = 0.05 to 0.30).
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the full form of BET analysis and how does the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller method work?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            The full form of BET analysis is Brunauer-Emmett-Teller analysis, named after Stephen Brunauer, Paul Emmett, and Edward Teller. It extends the Langmuir isotherm to account for multi-layer molecular gas adsorption on solid surfaces.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do I calculate specific surface area for activated carbon using BET characterization?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            To calculate specific surface area for activated carbon, acquire an N2 physisorption isotherm, select the appropriate linear pressure range using Rouquerol criteria to account for micropores, solve for monolayer capacity Vm from linear regression, and multiply by the nitrogen cross-sectional conversion factor.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the difference between the BET method and Langmuir adsorption models?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            While the Langmuir model assumes monolayer adsorption on energetically uniform sites without molecular interactions between adsorbed layers, the BET method permits multimolecular physical adsorption layers where subsequent molecules condense with liquid-like heat of vaporization.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do I evaluate multipoint adsorption data and Rouquerol criteria in a surface area analyzer?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Rouquerol consistency criteria require that the transformed function Va(1 - P/P0) increases monotonically with relative pressure and that the regression intercept yields a strictly positive C-constant, ensuring thermodynamic validity for microporous and mesoporous solids.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the acceptable relative pressure (P/P0) range for BET calculations?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            For classic mesoporous materials, the linear BET range is typically between P/P0 = 0.05 and 0.30. For microporous MOFs and zeolites, pore filling occurs at much lower pressures, shifting the valid linear region down to P/P0 = 0.005 to 0.08.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

    </article>
  </main>

